#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:4
msgid "For downstream package authors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:6
msgid ""
"This document aims to explain some best practices for authoring a package "
"that depends on NumPy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:11
msgid "Understanding NumPy's versioning and API/ABI stability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:13
msgid ""
"NumPy uses a standard, :pep:`440` compliant, versioning scheme: ``major."
"minor.bugfix``. A *major* release is highly unusual and if it happens it "
"will most likely indicate an ABI break. NumPy 1.xx releases happened from "
"2006 to 2023; NumPy 2.0 in early 2024 is the first release which changed the "
"ABI (minor ABI breaks for corner cases may have happened a few times in "
"minor releases). *Minor* versions are released regularly, typically every 6 "
"months. Minor versions contain new features, deprecations, and removals of "
"previously deprecated code. *Bugfix* releases are made even more frequently; "
"they do not contain any new features or deprecations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:24
msgid ""
"It is important to know that NumPy, like Python itself and most other well "
"known scientific Python projects, does **not** use semantic versioning. "
"Instead, backward incompatible API changes require deprecation warnings for "
"at least two releases. For more details, see :ref:`NEP23`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:29
msgid ""
"NumPy provides both a Python API and a C-API. The C-API can be accessed "
"directly or through tools like Cython or f2py. If your package uses the "
"NumPy C-API, it will generally be backward compatible with all relevant "
"older NumPy versions and forward compatible within the same major NumPy "
"version. For more details, for example if you wish to use API added in newer "
"NumPy versions, see :ref:`depending_on_numpy`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:36
msgid ""
"Modules can also be safely built against NumPy 2.0 or later in :ref:"
"`CPython's abi3 mode <python:stable-abi>`, which allows building against a "
"single (minimum-supported) version of Python but be forward compatible with "
"higher versions in the same series (e.g., ``3.x``). This can greatly reduce "
"the number of wheels that need to be built and distributed. For more "
"information and examples, see the `cibuildwheel docs <https://cibuildwheel."
"pypa.io/en/stable/faq/#abi3>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:47
msgid "Testing against the NumPy main branch or pre-releases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:49
msgid ""
"For large, actively maintained packages that depend on NumPy, we recommend "
"testing against the development version of NumPy in CI. To make this easy, "
"nightly builds are provided as wheels at https://anaconda.org/scientific-"
"python-nightly-wheels/. Example install command::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:54
msgid ""
"pip install -U --pre --only-binary :all: -i https://pypi.anaconda.org/"
"scientific-python-nightly-wheels/simple numpy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:56
msgid ""
"This helps detect regressions in NumPy that need fixing before the next "
"NumPy release.  Furthermore, we recommend to raise errors on warnings in CI "
"for this job, either all warnings or otherwise at least "
"``DeprecationWarning`` and ``FutureWarning``. This gives you an early "
"warning about changes in NumPy to adapt your code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:62
msgid ""
"If you want to test your own wheel builds against the latest NumPy nightly "
"build and you're using ``cibuildwheel``, you may need something like this in "
"your CI config file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:66
msgid ""
"CIBW_ENVIRONMENT: \"PIP_PRE=1 PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL=https://pypi.anaconda.org/"
"scientific-python-nightly-wheels/simple\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:74
msgid "Adding a dependency on NumPy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:77
msgid "Build-time dependency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:81
msgid ""
"Before NumPy 1.25, the NumPy C-API was *not* exposed in a backward "
"compatible way by default. This means that when compiling with a NumPy "
"version earlier than 1.25 you have to compile with the oldest version you "
"wish to support. This can be done by using `oldest-supported-numpy <https://"
"github.com/scipy/oldest-supported-numpy/>`__. Please see the `NumPy 1.24 "
"documentation <https://numpy.org/doc/1.24/dev/depending_on_numpy.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:90
msgid ""
"If a package either uses the NumPy C-API directly or it uses some other tool "
"that depends on it like Cython or Pythran, NumPy is a *build-time* "
"dependency of the package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:94
msgid ""
"By default, NumPy exposes an API that is backward compatible with the "
"earliest NumPy version that supports the oldest Python version currently "
"supported by NumPy. For example, NumPy 1.25.0 supports Python 3.9 and above; "
"and the earliest NumPy version to support Python 3.9 was 1.19. Therefore we "
"guarantee NumPy 1.25 will, when using defaults, expose a C-API compatible "
"with NumPy 1.19. (the exact version is set within NumPy-internal header "
"files)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:101
msgid ""
"NumPy is also forward compatible for all minor releases, but a major release "
"is expected to require recompilation (see :ref:`numpy-2-abi-handling` "
"further down).\\ [#major-transition]_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:105
msgid "The default behavior can be customized for example by adding::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:107
msgid "#define NPY_TARGET_VERSION NPY_1_22_API_VERSION"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:109
msgid ""
"before including any NumPy headers (or the equivalent ``-D`` compiler flag) "
"in every extension module that requires the NumPy C-API. This is mainly "
"useful if you need to use newly added API at the cost of not being "
"compatible with older versions.\\ [#future-api]_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:114
msgid ""
"If for some reason you wish to compile for the currently installed NumPy "
"version by default you can add::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:117
msgid ""
"#ifndef NPY_TARGET_VERSION\n"
"    #define NPY_TARGET_VERSION NPY_API_VERSION\n"
"#endif"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:121
msgid ""
"Which allows a user to override the default via ``-DNPY_TARGET_VERSION``. "
"This define must be consistent for each extension module (use of "
"``import_array()``) and also applies to the umath module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:125
msgid ""
"When you compile against NumPy, you should add the proper version "
"restrictions to your ``pyproject.toml`` (see PEP 517).  Since your extension "
"will not be compatible with a new major release of NumPy and may not be "
"compatible with very old versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:130
msgid ""
"For conda-forge packages, please see `here <https://conda-forge.org/docs/"
"maintainer/knowledge_base.html#building-against-numpy>`__ for instructions "
"on how to declare a dependency on ``numpy`` when using the C API."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:135
msgid ""
"Improving on the NumPy 2 transition, if a NumPy 3.0 release happens, we may "
"release NumPy 2.x versions that can compile modules that are compatible with "
"NumPy 3.0 when deprecated APIs are disabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:140
msgid ""
"We do not provide a mechanism to compile a single extension module that is "
"compatible with old NumPy versions but uses new NumPy API when running with "
"a newer NumPy version. This can be achieved via ``PyArray_RUNTIME_VERSION`` "
"and manually backported API, although you may not receive fixes from NumPy "
"headers when using a backport. We recommend only backporting when "
"``NPY_FEATURE_VERSION`` is lower than the version at which the API was "
"introduced. That way you pick up official header definitions and fixes once "
"they are available. Even without bugs, future NumPy versions could introduce "
"incompatible changes eventually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:154
msgid "Runtime dependency & version ranges"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:156
msgid ""
"NumPy itself and many core scientific Python packages have agreed on a "
"schedule for dropping support for old Python and NumPy versions: :ref:"
"`NEP29`. We recommend all packages depending on NumPy to follow the "
"recommendations in NEP 29."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:161
msgid ""
"For *run-time dependencies*, specify version bounds in `pyproject.toml`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:163
msgid ""
"Most libraries that rely on NumPy will not need to set an upper version "
"bound: NumPy is careful to preserve backward-compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:166
msgid ""
"That said, if you are (a) a project that is guaranteed to release "
"frequently, (b) use a large part of NumPy's API surface, and (c) is worried "
"that changes in NumPy may break your code, you can set an upper bound of "
"``<MAJOR.MINOR + N`` with N no less than 3, and ``MAJOR.MINOR`` being the "
"current release of NumPy.\\ [#N]_ If you use the NumPy C-API (directly or "
"via Cython), you can also pin the current major version to prevent ABI "
"breakage. Note that setting an upper bound on NumPy may `affect the ability "
"of your library to be installed alongside other, newer packages <https://"
"iscinumpy.dev/post/bound-version-constraints/>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:177
msgid ""
"The reason for setting ``N=3`` is that NumPy will, on the rare occasion "
"where it makes breaking changes, raise warnings for at least two releases. "
"(NumPy releases about once every six months, so this translates to a window "
"of at least a year; hence the subsequent requirement that your project "
"releases at least on that cadence.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:187
msgid ""
"SciPy has more documentation on how it builds wheels and deals with its "
"build-time and runtime dependencies `here <https://scipy.github.io/devdocs/"
"dev/core-dev/index.html#distributing>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:191
msgid ""
"NumPy and SciPy wheel build CI may also be useful as a reference, it can be "
"found `here for NumPy <https://github.com/MacPython/numpy-wheels>`__ and "
"`here for SciPy <https://github.com/MacPython/scipy-wheels>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:199
msgid "NumPy 2.0 ABI handling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:201
msgid "NumPy 2.0 changed the C ABI. The important rule for binary wheels is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:203
msgid ""
"Wheels built against NumPy 1.xx **will not work** with NumPy 2.0 or later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:204
msgid ""
"Wheels built against NumPy 2.x **will work** with NumPy 1.xx at runtime. How "
"old NumPy versions are supported can be customized with "
"``NPY_TARGET_VERSION``, see :ref:`depending_on_numpy`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:208
msgid ""
"If your package uses the NumPy C-API (directly or via Cython), you need to "
"rebuild and release wheels compiled against NumPy 2.x. Pure Python packages "
"may also need code updates; see :ref:`numpy-2-migration-guide`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:212
msgid "There are two common cases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:214
msgid "**Keep compatibility with NumPy 1.xx and 2.x**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:216
msgid ""
"Build against NumPy 2.x, but keep a lower runtime bound. For example, to "
"support NumPy 1.23.5 and up:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:219
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"build-backend = ...\n"
"requires = [\n"
"    \"numpy>=2.0\",\n"
"    ...\n"
"]\n"
"\n"
"[project]\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"numpy>=1.23.5\",\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:233
msgid "**Support NumPy 2.x only**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:235
msgid "This is simpler, but more restrictive for your users:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:237
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"build-backend = ...\n"
"requires = [\n"
"    \"numpy>=2.0\",\n"
"    ...\n"
"]\n"
"\n"
"[project]\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"numpy>=2.0\",\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:251
msgid ""
"We recommend at least one CI job that builds a wheel and then tests it "
"against the oldest NumPy version you support. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:254
msgid ""
"- name: Build wheel, then install it\n"
"  run: |\n"
"    python -m build\n"
"    python -m pip install dist/*.whl\n"
"\n"
"- name: Test against oldest supported NumPy version\n"
"  run: |\n"
"    python -m pip install numpy==1.23.5\n"
"    # now run test suite"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/depending_on_numpy.rst:266
msgid ""
"To test against unreleased NumPy versions, use a nightly build (see :ref:"
"`this section <testing-prereleases>`) or build NumPy from source."
msgstr ""
